    <info xml:lang="en">
      <p>To support the long-term preservation, accessibility, and reuse of
      deposited resources, LINDAT/CLARIAH-CZ recommends using open,
      well-documented, and widely supported file formats.</p>

      <p>The recommended formats are not mandatory. Depositors may submit data
      in other formats when they are appropriate for the research, but should
      provide sufficient documentation and, where possible, include an export
      or version in one of the preferred formats.</p>

      <p>The following principles guide our format recommendations:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Open standards are preferred to proprietary formats.</li>
        <li> Formats should be well documented and openly specified.</li>
        <li> Formats should be widely used, supported, and demonstrably
        stable.</li>
        <li> Text-based formats are preferred to binary formats where
        practical.</li>
        <li> Structured and machine-readable formats are preferred to
        application-specific formats.</li>
        <li> Data should use explicit character encoding, preferably
        UTF-8.</li>
        <li> Lossless compression is preferred for preservation copies of
        audio, images, and video.</li>
        <li> Files should be accompanied by documentation describing their
        structure, encoding, software dependencies, and any specialised
        terminology.</li>
        <li> Original files should be preserved when they are necessary for
        reproducibility, even if a preservation or dissemination copy is also
        provided.</li>
        <li> Files should not be encrypted or password-protected unless access
        restrictions require it.</li>
        <li> Archives should use formats that permit the inspection and
        extraction of their contents, such as ZIP or TAR/GZIP.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>LINDAT/CLARIAH-CZ encourages depositors to follow CLARIN
      recommendations and relevant community standards for language resources
      and digital humanities data. The repository’s preservation policy states
      that open, documented, verifiable, proven, and preferably text-based
      formats should be used; for digitised analogue material, lossless or
      uncompressed formats are recommended. </p>
    </info>
